encyclopedia :uluri do
  name "Uluri"
  summary "Uluri are small translucent swimmers from Lithren's enclosed brines that gather at warm surfaces and strike together when crowded."
  kind :lifeform
  subkind :animal
  status :complete
  topics :ecology, :danger, :subject_lithren
  prevalence :uncommon
  appears_when all: { place: [:outer_system, :cold] }
  biology "Finger-length swimmers with a muscular side fin, paired scraping jaws and heat-sensitive pits around the mouth"
  principal_accommodation "Warm saline water under retained pressure; strong flow breaks up a feeding group"
  descriptive_identity appearance: "Nearly clear bodies bend around a dark hooked gut; a silver line flashes along each side fin.", behavior: "Individuals converge on warmed surfaces, touch flanks and strike in a short coordinated burst.", threat: "Crowded swimmers can strip exposed soft tissue or chew a flexible sampling seal.", senses: "Heat gradients and contact with neighboring bodies direct feeding."
  prose <<~PROSE
    Uluri became known to excavators when a thawed well at #{ref :oravel} released a school into the sampling tray. They circled a heated probe, touched sides and struck it together. Their jaws scraped the probe's soft coating. A strong wash of brine scattered them into separate cracks, where they remained until the current slackened.

    They graze soft growth and hunt small waterborne animals. Warmth draws a school toward a likely feeding surface, but a sufficiently hot object drives it back. Keepers use a mildly warmed stone inside a mesh sleeve to collect them without offering skin. The group response travels through touching bodies; separated swimmers continue searching independently. A few specimens can disappear into tubing too narrow for a person's finger.

    Escaped uluri at Oravel reached a shelter's warmed drainage and began tapping beneath its sleeping occupants. Sealed displays in #{ref :ithara} use broad outlets with removable mesh instead of inaccessible narrow pipes. The #{ref :ithara_comparative_expedition, "Ithara Comparative Expedition"}'s drawings show both an isolated swimmer and a feeding knot, which otherwise look like different animals through cloudy water. Uluri carried away from Lithren remain dependent on a suitable enclosed pool; drying and open-surface exposure are lethal.
  PROSE
  cue "A silver knot forms beneath the warm cup, strikes its base and comes apart into transparent swimmers."
  cue "Light taps travel beneath a sleeping platform whenever someone rolls onto the heated side."
  affordance "A warm lure in a mesh sleeve draws swimmers out of a flooded compartment without draining their habitat."
  variation "Well specimens gather in dense schools that can be scattered by a short strong current."
  variation "Animals established in shelter plumbing emerge in small groups from several outlets, making one cleared drain an unreliable sign of an empty system."
end
